# Every layer whose holders' shares are all stated, beside the magnitude they must add up to. One
# row per layer examined, `violates` where the sum misses.
#
# The shares add up to the magnitude
#   The rule: the stated shares add up to the size of the remainder. Between them, the holders
#   account for the whole of what there was to bear, no more and no less.
#
# One unstated share suspends the check
#   It does not break it, and the difference matters. If a filer says one party's share is
#   `unmeasured`, the sum is unknown, not wrong. Treating the blank as a zero would make up a
#   shortfall and report the document for a defect the check itself put there.
#
# The unit guard is half of this rule
#   arithmetic/shares_sum.sqlc reports `not comparable` where a holder's share is quoted in a unit
#   other than its layer's, and this rule examines only layers whose shares are all in the layer's
#   unit, rather than adding across units. A share in a foreign unit is not a smaller share; it is
#   not a share of this remainder at all. arithmetic/roster.sqlc records no unit rule at that site,
#   so this check is the only thing between it and a wrong number. The two agree on this corpus
#   because no document quotes a share in a foreign unit yet.
#
# The whole range, not the middle value
#   A quantity here is a range, so the shares are compared with the magnitude at all three points,
#   not only at the most likely one.
#
# Inside at the ends, equal at the most likely value
#   The shares' lowest and highest values must lie within the magnitude's, and their most likely
#   value must equal it. Requiring equality at the ends would accuse a more careful filing. The
#   magnitude is read from layers/remainder.sqlc, the remainder in force, so on a composed layer it
#   is the one worked out from the parts, not from the composed totals. That is why
#   `merge-holding-composition/compute`'s shares, 1414, 2857 and 4085.6, equal its magnitude at all
#   three points. Its composed totals would give 1092, 2857 and 4198.8, looser at both ends, and a
#   rule reading them would accept shares the remainder does not allow.
#
# A remainder that runs from short to spare
#   Where the remainder runs from short to spare, its smallest size is zero: short at the top of the
#   demand range and spare at the bottom, both at once, which is the transition fit. Taking the
#   smaller of the two ends there would invent a floor the layer does not have.
#
# Probe
#   Widen `enterprise-contract/support-cover`'s only share from 21, 50 and 76 to 21, 50 and 300, on
#   a layer whose whole nameplate is 168, leaving the most likely value where it is. This rule must
#   fire.
-- pm:Remainder/pm:holder summed against the size of the remainder in layers/remainder.sqlc,
-- via entries/holder_totals.sqlc.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT x.filing, x.layer,
           abs(x.shares - x.magnitude) > 1e-9
           OR x.shares_low  < x.mag_low  - 1e-9
           OR x.shares_high > x.mag_high + 1e-9                       AS violates,
           format('shares [%s, %s, %s] against a magnitude of [%s, %s, %s]',
                  x.shares_low, x.shares, x.shares_high,
                  x.mag_low, x.magnitude, x.mag_high)                 AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT r.filing, r.layer,
               r.m_mode       AS magnitude,
               h.shares_mode  AS shares,
               h.shares_low, h.shares_high,
               r.m_low        AS mag_low,
               r.m_high       AS mag_high
        FROM      (
            :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
        ) r
        JOIN      (
            :compose(entries/holder_totals.sqlc)
        ) h USING (filing, layer)
        WHERE h.unstated = 0
          AND h.share_units = ARRAY[r.unit]
    ) x
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'shares_do_not_sum'
